263
00:10:44,178 --> 00:10:46,480
It's a Warhol series.

264
00:10:46,513 --> 00:10:48,148
What is your report?

265
00:10:48,182 --> 00:10:51,118
I wish to report to
the intergalactic council

266
00:10:51,152 --> 00:10:54,155
that my carrier has arrived
on station as programmed.

267
00:10:54,188 --> 00:10:55,455
I await further orders.

268
00:10:55,488 --> 00:10:56,590
Clear your throat.

269
00:10:56,623 --> 00:10:59,927
You may now be advised
of your assignment.

270
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:03,997
The primary objective of
your mission to planet Earth

271
00:11:04,032 --> 00:11:06,267
is to establish a colony within

272
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:09,002
the advanced society
of the planet.

273
00:11:09,037 --> 00:11:10,037
Okay.

274
00:11:10,070 --> 00:11:14,208
This is necessary in
order that we may expand

275
00:11:14,242 --> 00:11:18,845
our galaxy domination
program in the near future.

276
00:11:18,878 --> 00:11:21,048
I understand the
importance of the mission.

277
00:11:21,082 --> 00:11:21,982
Yeah right.

278
00:11:22,015 --> 00:11:22,950
You will accomplish

279
00:11:22,983 --> 00:11:25,285
this infiltration
of the Earth society

280
00:11:25,318 --> 00:11:27,988
with the aid of Professor
Vaughn Dornheimer,

281
00:11:28,022 --> 00:11:28,988
(audience laughing)

282
00:11:29,022 --> 00:11:31,125
a renowned scientist
of that society.

283
00:11:31,158 --> 00:11:32,025
And a good friend.

284
00:11:32,058 --> 00:11:33,693
I understand, master.

285
00:11:33,727 --> 00:11:37,865
If you are detected,
if you fail in any way

286
00:11:37,898 --> 00:11:39,200
It will go on your
permanent record.

287
00:11:39,233 --> 00:11:41,035
You will be destroyed.

288
00:11:41,068 --> 00:11:42,870
I will not fail.

289
00:11:42,903 --> 00:11:44,272
Much.

290
00:11:44,305 --> 00:11:47,307
Agent Kolos, you may
now take your place

291
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:50,210
before the teletransporter.

292
00:11:50,243 --> 00:11:52,847
We will contact you
after you've had

293
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:55,883
sufficient time to
accomplish your mission.

294
00:11:55,917 --> 00:11:56,883
Okay? Buh-bye now.

295
00:11:56,917 --> 00:11:59,287
Buh-bye.

296
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,122
Your celebrity partner
will be Nipsey Russel!

297
00:12:02,155 --> 00:12:03,323
(cheering)

298
00:12:03,357 --> 00:12:07,428
Sprinkle a day makes
Richard Kiel go away.

299
00:12:07,462 --> 00:12:11,332
Did I leave the iron on?

300
00:12:11,365 --> 00:12:15,970
Oh no, he got beamed into
the "Song of the South".

301
00:12:16,003 --> 00:12:18,105
♪ Zip a dee do dah

302
00:12:18,138 --> 00:12:19,640
I'm huge.

303
00:12:19,673 --> 00:12:21,875
We visited Anthony Robbins

304
00:12:21,908 --> 00:12:30,050
in his palatial home in Del Mar.

305
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:32,487
I begin my tour of
Southern California

306
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:34,922
with a quick trip to Greystone.

307
00:12:34,955 --> 00:12:36,590
This is "Beverly Hills Cop",

308
00:12:36,623 --> 00:12:39,627
only the slow, white version.

309
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:41,095
So, uh, what is his mission?

310
00:12:41,128 --> 00:12:42,428
I dunno.

311
00:12:42,463 --> 00:12:44,832
Selling band candy or something.

312
00:12:44,865 --> 00:12:46,000
You rang?

313
00:12:46,033 --> 00:12:50,137
Oh wait, I'm not
Ted Cassidy, sorry.

314
00:12:50,170 --> 00:12:51,005
(audience yelling)

315
00:12:51,038 --> 00:12:52,773
Can I help you, sir?

316
00:12:52,807 --> 00:12:54,508
I wish to see
Professor Dornheimer.

317
00:12:54,542 --> 00:12:56,943
I'm very sorry sir, the
professor's very busy.

318
00:12:56,977 --> 00:12:58,878
You'll have to make
an appointment.

319
00:12:58,912 --> 00:13:01,215
I wish to see the
professor at once.

320
00:13:01,248 --> 00:13:02,517
(dramatic music)

321
00:13:02,550 --> 00:13:05,853
(audience mimicking choking)

322
00:13:05,887 --> 00:13:10,357
He must have read
"Getting to Yes."

323
00:13:10,390 --> 00:13:15,795
Looks like the Ponderosa.

324
00:13:15,830 --> 00:13:20,633
Excuse me, where's
the little giants' room?

325
00:13:20,667 --> 00:13:25,138
(Crow singing
"Bonanza" theme song)

326
00:13:25,172 --> 00:13:27,107
Oh? Hmm.

327
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:31,312
(gentle piano music)

328
00:13:31,345 --> 00:13:34,182
Kate Mulgrew is Mrs. Chopin.

329
00:13:34,215 --> 00:13:36,150
Uh, dear Penthouse,

330
00:13:36,183 --> 00:13:41,355
I am a child from a small
Midwestern satellite, and, uh...

331
00:13:41,388 --> 00:13:45,758
I'm here for my piano
lesson, Madame Sousatzka.

332
00:13:45,792 --> 00:13:47,595
Oh Silky, have you
been rolling in the carp?

333
00:13:47,628 --> 00:13:49,095
Thor?

334
00:13:49,128 --> 00:13:50,998
Is that you?

335
00:13:51,032 --> 00:13:52,365
Oh, she's blind.

336
00:13:52,398 --> 00:13:53,633
Well that explains
the decorating.

337
00:13:53,667 --> 00:13:54,502
(Tom laughing nervously)

338
00:13:54,535 --> 00:13:56,137
Is that you professor?

339
00:13:56,170 --> 00:13:58,805
Uh no, it's me, Gilligan.

340
00:13:58,838 --> 00:14:02,208
Hey, who knows where
that hand has been!

341
00:14:02,242 --> 00:14:03,543
Sleep!

342
00:14:03,577 --> 00:14:04,278
Oh well.

343
00:14:04,312 --> 00:14:05,278
Who is it?

344
00:14:05,312 --> 00:14:06,813
Who's there?

345
00:14:06,847 --> 00:14:08,815
I'm Dr. Kolos.

346
00:14:08,848 --> 00:14:11,018
A colleague of
Professor Dornheimer.

347
00:14:11,052 --> 00:14:13,053
I have traveled a great
distance to see him.

348
00:14:13,087 --> 00:14:14,722
Geez, somebody
get her a sandwich.

349
00:14:14,755 --> 00:14:16,690
The professor is
in his laboratory.

350
00:14:16,723 --> 00:14:18,358
Was he expecting you, Doctor?

351
00:14:18,392 --> 00:14:20,627
No, he was not
expecting my visit.

352
00:14:20,660 --> 00:14:22,095
Oh, but he'll be
pleasantly surprised.

353
00:14:22,128 --> 00:14:25,032
Well, he doesn't like to be
disturbed while he's working.

354
00:14:25,065 --> 00:14:27,367
Perhaps you could phone in the
morning for an appointment?

355
00:14:27,400 --> 00:14:28,835
Or if you care to wait--

356
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:30,703
It is imperative
that I see him at once.

357
00:14:30,737 --> 00:14:32,138
It's a stripogram.

358
00:14:32,172 --> 00:14:35,275
I assure you, it
will be alright.

359
00:14:35,308 --> 00:14:38,178
My business with your uncle
is of extreme importance.

360
00:14:38,212 --> 00:14:39,480
Come on, I'm huge, humor me.

361
00:14:39,513 --> 00:14:41,648
You may trust in me, Lisa.

362
00:14:41,682 --> 00:14:43,350
How did you know my name?

363
00:14:43,383 --> 00:14:45,885
I have been informed
of your presence here.

364
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:48,755
But I had not expected
you to be so beautiful.

365
00:14:48,788 --> 00:14:50,992
Look, I'm from another
planet, the meter's running.

366
00:14:51,025 --> 00:14:54,295
I will show you to the
laboratory, Dr. Kolos.

367
00:14:54,328 --> 00:15:02,135
(audience hooting
and catcalling)

368
00:15:05,005 --> 00:15:08,342
It's quite alright,
Doctor, I can find my way.

369
00:15:08,375 --> 00:15:10,277
You see, I grew up here.

370
00:15:10,310 --> 00:15:15,882
I know every inch of
this house by heart.

371
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:18,118
Ooh, new inch.

372
00:15:18,152 --> 00:15:20,487
Uh, I guess you know
that you're leading me

373
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:21,788
into the bathroom, ha ha.

374
00:15:21,822 --> 00:15:24,023
It's the blind
leading the bland.

375
00:15:24,057 --> 00:15:25,492
And this is the kitchen,

376
00:15:25,525 --> 00:15:27,628
here's the new stove we
just ordered from Sears.

377
00:15:27,662 --> 00:15:31,532
(dramatic music)

378
00:15:31,565 --> 00:15:35,768
And, uh, this is my room,
it's done in pinks.

379
00:15:35,802 --> 00:15:40,773
Marilyn's date is
here and he's ugly!

380
00:15:40,807 --> 00:15:45,980
This is my velvet
underground, like it?

381
00:15:46,013 --> 00:15:50,217
Looks like they forgot
to strike the time tunnel set.

382
00:15:50,250 --> 00:15:51,852
Oh great, look who came in,

383
00:15:51,885 --> 00:15:54,187
and without an
appointment, I might add.

384
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:55,688
It's Grant Tinker!

385
00:15:55,722 --> 00:16:00,193
(dramatic music)

386
00:16:00,227 --> 00:16:02,763
Professor Dornheimer.

387
00:16:02,797 --> 00:16:04,798
I am Dr. Kolos.

388
00:16:04,832 --> 00:16:08,335
The God of Hellfire
and I bring you...

389
00:16:08,368 --> 00:16:10,303
(audience yelling)

390
00:16:10,337 --> 00:16:11,838
Oh my God, you're huge.

391
00:16:11,872 --> 00:16:14,107
I'm sure I do
not know you, sir.

392
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:15,442
But I think I could like you.

393
00:16:15,475 --> 00:16:16,877
Perhaps you made a mistake.

394
00:16:16,910 --> 00:16:19,813
There is no mistake,
Professor Dornheimer.

395
00:16:19,847 --> 00:16:22,382
I have come to assist
you in your experiment.

396
00:16:22,415 --> 00:16:23,450
Uh, but we're not hiring.

397
00:16:23,483 --> 00:16:27,320
I don't need your assistance.

398
00:16:27,353 --> 00:16:28,655
(laughing) But then again,

399
00:16:28,688 --> 00:16:32,993
I could use a huge
man and a, oh.

400
00:16:33,027 --> 00:16:35,128
But you do, sir.

401
00:16:35,162 --> 00:16:37,430
Your theories are correct.

402
00:16:37,463 --> 00:16:39,600
But your technique is wrong.

403
00:16:39,633 --> 00:16:40,500
Everything you know is wrong!

404
00:16:40,533 --> 00:16:42,468
In this manner,

405
00:16:42,502 --> 00:16:45,772
you will not reach your
goal for many years.

406
00:16:45,805 --> 00:16:47,708
Together, professor,

407
00:16:47,742 --> 00:16:52,545
we shall create an
android in a few days.

408
00:16:52,578 --> 00:16:54,448
Besides, I brought
my own lab coat.

409
00:16:54,482 --> 00:16:55,915
I have important work to do.

410
00:16:55,948 --> 00:16:58,318
I will not be distracted
by a total stranger

411
00:16:58,352 --> 00:17:00,087
with fantastic ideas.

412
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:01,288
Thanks, I think.

413
00:17:01,322 --> 00:17:02,655
But you will, sir.

414
00:17:02,688 --> 00:17:03,590
(dramatic music)

415
00:17:03,623 --> 00:17:06,693
Pons and Fleischmann,
the early years.

416
00:17:06,727 --> 00:17:08,295
I command it.

417
00:17:08,328 --> 00:17:09,630
Oh, well in that case.

418
00:17:09,663 --> 00:17:11,332
Who are you?

419
00:17:11,365 --> 00:17:12,767
I am Kolos.

420
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,000
And Diet Kolos.

421
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:16,035
(dramatic music)

422
00:17:16,070 --> 00:17:18,038
Kevin McHale, no!

423
00:17:18,072 --> 00:17:19,707
I am your master.

424
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:20,607
(sudden trumpeting)

425
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,642
Oh, brother's got
his own horn section.

426
00:17:22,675 --> 00:17:24,310
Cool.

427
00:17:24,343 --> 00:17:30,483
("Love Theme from MST3K")

428
00:17:30,517 --> 00:17:36,423
Next week on
"Human Duplicators".

429
00:17:36,457 --> 00:17:38,325
Good morning sir.

430
00:17:38,358 --> 00:17:41,795
Ah, sit and spin.

431
00:17:41,828 --> 00:17:47,267
Kind of a high-security
Budgetel here, huh?

432
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:51,003
Oh he's lucky to get
a parking spot so close.

433
00:17:51,037 --> 00:17:52,605
He's got special clearance

434
00:17:52,638 --> 00:17:54,442
for the hospitality suite.

435
00:17:54,475 --> 00:17:55,708
Well, you know, he
could have walked down

436
00:17:55,742 --> 00:17:58,378
those high security
steps over there.

437
00:17:58,412 --> 00:17:59,947
Yes, well, I worked at NASA

438
00:17:59,980 --> 00:18:07,120
back when we were
next to Cost Cutters!

439
00:18:07,153 --> 00:18:08,488
Dr. Munson, good morning, sir.

440
00:18:08,522 --> 00:18:10,590
Hey, it's the
Maytag repairman.

441
00:18:10,623 --> 00:18:13,127
Came in a little
early, couldn't sleep?

442
00:18:13,160 --> 00:18:16,095
I'm here for the Mr.
Drysdale audition. (laughing)

443
00:18:16,130 --> 00:18:18,298
That's right, couldn't sleep.

444
00:18:18,332 --> 00:18:19,132
Perchance to dream.

445
00:18:19,165 --> 00:18:20,200
Oh, wait, sir,

446
00:18:20,233 --> 00:18:23,803
I'm sorry, Doctor, but you
know our security rules.

447
00:18:23,837 --> 00:18:25,005
Yes, of course.

448
00:18:25,038 --> 00:18:27,875
Hop like a bunny.

449
00:18:27,908 --> 00:18:28,875
Okay, let's see.

450
00:18:28,908 --> 00:18:34,113
Woozle, whose name is
Peanut, there you go.

451
00:18:34,147 --> 00:18:35,282
There, is that better?

452
00:18:35,315 --> 00:18:37,050
Yes sir, thank you.

453
00:18:37,083 --> 00:18:38,852
Wait, sit and spin,
hey wait a minute!

454
00:18:38,885 --> 00:18:40,187
Computer room, eh?

455
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:44,357
Boing.

456
00:18:44,390 --> 00:18:46,025
There better be a
continental breakfast,

457
00:18:46,058 --> 00:18:47,862
that's all I gotta say.

458
00:18:47,895 --> 00:18:49,663
Why did he have to
sign in to go outside?

459
00:18:49,697 --> 00:18:51,030
It's California.

460
00:18:51,063 --> 00:18:54,000
(dramatic music)

461
00:18:54,033 --> 00:18:56,737
Oh, boy. (laughing)
That makes it good.

462
00:18:56,770 --> 00:18:58,572
It's "The Pawnbroker" meets

463
00:18:58,605 --> 00:19:03,443
"It's a Mad, Mad, Mad, Mad
World," the restored version.

464
00:19:03,477 --> 00:19:05,945
Hmm.

465
00:19:05,978 --> 00:19:08,115
All I have to do is... hey.

466
00:19:08,148 --> 00:19:09,517
Wait a minute.

467
00:19:09,550 --> 00:19:11,050
I only have so many years left,

468
00:19:11,083 --> 00:19:12,552
why am I wasting
my time in this film?

469
00:19:12,585 --> 00:19:13,287
Agh!

470
00:19:13,320 --> 00:19:14,220
(alarm sounding)

471
00:19:14,253 --> 00:19:16,457
Hey, Fred Flinstone's
running somewhere.

472
00:19:16,490 --> 00:19:17,257
(Tom impersonating
Flinstones' car motor)

473
00:19:17,290 --> 00:19:19,827
I wet 'em!

474
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:23,497
Oh, got to get to bio!

475
00:19:23,530 --> 00:19:25,032
This is what they
get for leaving

476
00:19:25,065 --> 00:19:28,835
national security secrets in
a high school in Pasadena.

477
00:19:28,868 --> 00:19:32,772
It's the office of Daniel
Ellsberg's electrician.

478
00:19:32,805 --> 00:19:34,775
I'll stand still,
he won't see me.

479
00:19:34,808 --> 00:19:35,608
Oh, Dr. Munson!

480
00:19:35,642 --> 00:19:36,777
Where's your AV card?

481
00:19:36,810 --> 00:19:39,445
I thought you were
in the computer room.

482
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:40,947
What happened here?

483
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:43,850
Nothing to be
concerned about, Johnson.

484
00:19:43,883 --> 00:19:45,552
- It's Olson.
- Nothing whatever.

485
00:19:45,585 --> 00:19:47,853
Glad everything's alright!

486
00:19:47,888 --> 00:19:50,823
Sounds like a promotion.

487
00:19:50,857 --> 00:19:54,795
Dr. Heimlich, the early years.

488
00:19:54,828 --> 00:19:57,863
Now I can take all
the penny nails I want.

489
00:19:57,898 --> 00:19:59,198
(evil laughing)

490
00:19:59,232 --> 00:20:01,868
(dramatic music)

491
00:20:01,902 --> 00:20:07,607
All classes dismissed,
school's out forever!

492
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:10,643
Hey, look, there's
a doorknob, buddy!

493
00:20:10,677 --> 00:20:12,378
Jerk.

494
00:20:12,412 --> 00:20:13,513
Dr. Munson!

495
00:20:13,547 --> 00:20:15,182
I think I love you!

496
00:20:15,215 --> 00:20:17,550
(gunfire)

497
00:20:17,583 --> 00:20:18,652
(gunfire)

498
00:20:18,685 --> 00:20:22,355
Greenie's Stick-M-Caps
won't stop him!

499
00:20:22,388 --> 00:20:23,790
Alright, okay, forget it,

500
00:20:23,823 --> 00:20:25,958
I'll sign you out then,
okay, bye.

501
00:20:25,992 --> 00:20:30,863
Stop in the name of
Tom Bodett!

502
00:20:30,897 --> 00:20:32,465
(mimicking police radio)
1-Adam-12, 1-Adam-12...

503
00:20:32,498 --> 00:20:39,305
See the man, government secrets
stolen from the Motel 6.

504
00:20:39,338 --> 00:20:41,275
You know, there's one thing
I can't stand, Steve.

505
00:20:41,308 --> 00:20:44,945
- What's that, Wayne?
- Towel thieves.

506
00:20:45,012 --> 00:20:50,950
Need a room, please.

507
00:20:51,017 --> 00:20:52,652
Tenspeed and Brown Shoe!

508
00:20:52,718 --> 00:20:54,120
National Intelligence Agency.

509
00:20:54,153 --> 00:20:57,823
- NIA, this might be a story!
- Wish we were reporters.

510
00:20:57,857 --> 00:21:00,160
You tell me. I only know
what I read in your papers.

511
00:21:00,227 --> 00:21:01,528
Well, what does he mean
by that?

512
00:21:01,528 --> 00:21:04,430
It's simple. Something's up,
so he doesn't wanna talk.

513
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:06,433
They never do,
those guys from NIA

514
00:21:06,467 --> 00:21:07,833
Might as well forget about it.

515
00:21:07,833 --> 00:21:10,137
Oh, not me! If there's a
story here, I'm gonna get it!

516
00:21:10,170 --> 00:21:11,438
With my accordion!

517
00:21:11,438 --> 00:21:12,772
Sure.

518
00:21:12,838 --> 00:21:15,175
Officer Krupke,
I'm down on my knees...

519
00:21:15,242 --> 00:21:17,443
Thank you, constable.

520
00:21:17,443 --> 00:21:19,078
Oh, he's a quart low.

521
00:21:19,145 --> 00:21:20,447
Why should I hurry?

522
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:22,048
You're doing my work for me.

523
00:21:22,048 --> 00:21:23,950
Mickey Spillane?

524
00:21:23,983 --> 00:21:25,152
I can't have a
quiet homicide anymore

525
00:21:25,152 --> 00:21:27,553
without you NIA
boys being involved.

526
00:21:27,553 --> 00:21:29,455
You pull that suit
off the Riddler?

527
00:21:29,455 --> 00:21:31,992
We're dealing with
an espionage ring.

528
00:21:32,058 --> 00:21:33,393
It might be just that, Shaw.

529
00:21:33,460 --> 00:21:34,360
Dick Clark!

530
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:35,862
What'd they take this time?

531
00:21:35,862 --> 00:21:38,265
Same as before,
transistors, dials,

532
00:21:38,298 --> 00:21:40,667
laser crystals,
things like that.

533
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:43,670
I can't believe I loved him.

534
00:21:43,670 --> 00:21:45,172
And the suspect?

535
00:21:45,172 --> 00:21:47,007
Eh, seemed like a nice guy.

536
00:21:47,073 --> 00:21:48,308
Trusted member of the staff,

537
00:21:48,375 --> 00:21:50,477
character above
suspicion as usual?

538
00:21:50,477 --> 00:21:53,280
Same, Dr. Munson,
head physicist.

539
00:21:53,280 --> 00:21:55,882
Three times in the
past three months.

540
00:21:55,915 --> 00:21:57,783
- That adds up to something...
- Well here he is!

541
00:21:57,817 --> 00:21:59,920
...like espionage in our books.

542
00:21:59,987 --> 00:22:02,088
Uh, you're
kneeling on his head.

543
00:22:02,122 --> 00:22:04,223
Looks like this Dr. Munson
was the strong man type.

544
00:22:04,290 --> 00:22:05,225
Dr. Munson Honeydew.

545
00:22:05,292 --> 00:22:06,092
You're wrong.

546
00:22:06,125 --> 00:22:07,627
I've got news for you,
Glenn boy,

547
00:22:07,693 --> 00:22:09,930
that man's some
kind of a superman.

548
00:22:09,997 --> 00:22:13,200
The guard outside fired four
shots at him, point blank,

549
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:14,500
never even fazed him.

550
00:22:14,533 --> 00:22:16,302
And to top that,
he busted through

551
00:22:16,335 --> 00:22:18,003
a two inch door out there.

552
00:22:18,038 --> 00:22:19,505
Drinkin' before noon again?

553
00:22:19,505 --> 00:22:21,742
Hate to be the man that
tries to bring him in.

554
00:22:21,808 --> 00:22:23,810
I want to take a
look at his files.

555
00:22:23,843 --> 00:22:25,412
Where's his office?

556
00:22:25,445 --> 00:22:26,813
Across the hall,
I'll show you.

557
00:22:26,847 --> 00:22:29,248
Next to the ice machine.

558
00:22:29,315 --> 00:22:30,617
Lieutenant Shaw?

559
00:22:30,617 --> 00:22:33,153
The highway patrol is
calling in from Stone Canyon.

560
00:22:33,220 --> 00:22:35,155
Eric Estrada is on his way.

561
00:22:35,222 --> 00:22:36,623
(dramatic music)

562
00:22:36,657 --> 00:22:42,562
Hmm. This wheat looks
just about ready to harvest.

563
00:22:42,628 --> 00:22:45,532
Who you gonna call?
(laughs)

564
00:22:45,532 --> 00:22:52,938
Space Research Corporation
and Motor Lodge.

565
00:22:52,972 --> 00:22:54,440
Get me Broderick Crawford.

566
00:22:54,473 --> 00:22:55,842
This is Shaw,
let's have it.

567
00:22:55,875 --> 00:22:58,245
Lieutenant, we found the car,
but your man's vanished.

568
00:22:58,278 --> 00:22:59,645
For the moment, anyway.

569
00:22:59,645 --> 00:23:02,615
We've checked and double-checked
the canyon road, not a trace.

570
00:23:02,648 --> 00:23:05,685
Well, keep checking.
Can't get far, he's wounded.

571
00:23:05,718 --> 00:23:07,320
We won't be able
to search too well

572
00:23:07,353 --> 00:23:09,355
until the morning
fog lifts, sir.

573
00:23:09,388 --> 00:23:10,723
Well, do the best you can.

574
00:23:10,757 --> 00:23:12,425
My wife.

575
00:23:12,458 --> 00:23:13,493
He doesn't have
much of a chance.

576
00:23:13,527 --> 00:23:14,995
Don't take bets on it.

577
00:23:15,028 --> 00:23:16,663
Come on, let's take a look
at those files.

578
00:23:16,697 --> 00:23:18,598
Hey, it's Foreman and Clark!

579
00:23:18,632 --> 00:23:20,800
Who did what to whom,
and why?

580
00:23:20,833 --> 00:23:22,502
You'll have to ask him,
it's his case.

581
00:23:22,535 --> 00:23:24,170
I'm just here to assist.

582
00:23:24,203 --> 00:23:26,138
Here we go again, kid.
The old ball game.

583
00:23:26,172 --> 00:23:27,940
Alright, Mr. Martin,
what's the score?

584
00:23:27,973 --> 00:23:30,110
You want all the facts and
nothing but the facts, right?

585
00:23:30,143 --> 00:23:31,445
(all)
So help us God.

586
00:23:31,478 --> 00:23:32,812
My office will issue a
statement for the press

587
00:23:32,845 --> 00:23:34,280
as soon as we have those facts.

588
00:23:34,313 --> 00:23:35,482
Come on,
give us a break, will ya?

589
00:23:35,515 --> 00:23:38,652
Sorry, that's the way it is.

590
00:23:38,685 --> 00:23:41,988
Say, are those
Bugle Boy jeans?

591
00:23:42,022 --> 00:23:45,958
And so, Hercules entered
the realm of the Minotaur.

592
00:23:45,992 --> 00:23:49,128
I think it's my
battery again.

593
00:23:49,162 --> 00:23:52,998
Uh, hope you brought the buns.

594
00:23:53,032 --> 00:23:56,737
- Any way to get down there?
- Uh, donkeys?

595
00:23:56,770 --> 00:23:58,738
It's quite a hike, though.

596
00:23:58,772 --> 00:23:59,873
Whose place is that?

597
00:23:59,907 --> 00:24:02,842
I think I know, his house
is in the village, though.

598
00:24:02,875 --> 00:24:05,612
There's a funny old guy,
wants complete privacy.

599
00:24:05,645 --> 00:24:07,280
Supposed to be a famous
doctor or something.

600
00:24:07,313 --> 00:24:08,482
Well, takes all kinds.
(laughs)

601
00:24:08,515 --> 00:24:11,952
Let's go.

602
00:24:11,985 --> 00:24:14,487
Can I ride your back?
I wanna ride on your back.

603
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:21,093
C'mon.

604
00:24:21,127 --> 00:24:22,995
Bobby? Cindy?

605
00:24:23,028 --> 00:24:25,965
To be continued.

606
00:24:25,998 --> 00:24:29,402
Hey, let's ask this
old guy taking a nap.

607
00:24:29,435 --> 00:24:33,072
Ooh, kids come down
here and drink lime vodka.

608
00:24:33,105 --> 00:24:34,507
I guess he sort of lost his way.

609
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:35,808
In all that fog this morning,

610
00:24:35,842 --> 00:24:37,143
he couldn't see
the edge of the cliff.

611
00:24:37,177 --> 00:24:40,580
We gotta go.

612
00:24:40,613 --> 00:24:43,115
Come on mister,
you can't sleep here.

613
00:24:43,148 --> 00:24:47,353
(dramatic music)

614
00:24:47,387 --> 00:24:49,155
No Munson.

615
00:24:49,188 --> 00:24:56,897
(doors creak and whir)

616
00:24:56,930 --> 00:24:59,532
Okay, everybody ready
with the big craft project?

617
00:24:59,565 --> 00:25:00,400
- Yeah.
- Uh huh.

618
00:25:00,433 --> 00:25:02,835
Oh yes, yes, yes, yes, oh.

619
00:25:02,868 --> 00:25:03,903
- Alright.
- Oh yes, yes.

620
00:25:03,937 --> 00:25:05,905
Okay and that
craft project was?

621
00:25:05,938 --> 00:25:09,075
To make our very own spaceship

622
00:25:09,108 --> 00:25:13,747
that looks just as good as
the one in today's experiment

623
00:25:13,780 --> 00:25:17,850
out of everyday things
you find around the house.

624
00:25:17,883 --> 00:25:19,953
Exactly, and who
wants to go first?

625
00:25:19,987 --> 00:25:21,420
Oh! Oh!

626
00:25:21,453 --> 00:25:23,690
- I'll go first, I'll go.
- Okay, Gypsy.

627
00:25:23,723 --> 00:25:25,925
You go first, alright
this is yours, boy,

628
00:25:25,958 --> 00:25:28,328
looks like you put a lot of
work into it, what'd you use?

629
00:25:28,362 --> 00:25:30,597
I used a Quaker Oats box,

630
00:25:30,630 --> 00:25:31,632
Uh huh.

631
00:25:31,665 --> 00:25:33,900
- Some of my old earrings.
- Mm-hmm.

632
00:25:33,933 --> 00:25:35,968
A sneaker and some
carpet pieces left over

633
00:25:36,002 --> 00:25:38,972
from when I single-handedly
remodeled my room.

634
00:25:39,005 --> 00:25:40,773
Oh, that is great.

635
00:25:40,807 --> 00:25:42,342
You know what? I'm gonna put
that on the refrigerator.

636
00:25:42,375 --> 00:25:43,443
Oh!

637
00:25:43,477 --> 00:25:44,810
And we have Tom's over here.

638
00:25:44,843 --> 00:25:48,013
Looks like he's got a
Clorox bottle, is it?

639
00:25:48,047 --> 00:25:49,748
Yeah, it's a
spaceship but I suppose

640
00:25:49,782 --> 00:25:52,785
you could use it as a rock salt
scoop or whatever, I dunno.

641
00:25:52,818 --> 00:25:54,922
Doesn't sound like
you're too into it, Tom.

642
00:25:54,955 --> 00:25:57,390
Okay, draw two nose holes
on it, it's a piggy bank.

643
00:25:57,423 --> 00:26:00,927
Oh Crow T. Robot,
would you look at this?

644
00:26:00,960 --> 00:26:02,162
Wow.

645
00:26:02,195 --> 00:26:03,597
This is exemplary, Crow.

646
00:26:03,630 --> 00:26:06,265
It's a scale model of
the Satellite of Love

647
00:26:06,298 --> 00:26:09,002
and it is fantastic,
how did you do it, Crow?

648
00:26:09,035 --> 00:26:12,738
String, toothpicks, eggshells
and baking powder paste.

649
00:26:12,772 --> 00:26:15,008
I'm gonna put lighter fluid on
it and burn it in the driveway.

650
00:26:15,042 --> 00:26:17,643
Well, it just goes to
show you that you can make

651
00:26:17,677 --> 00:26:19,445
a spaceship model
out of anything.

652
00:26:19,478 --> 00:26:21,080
Here's mine right here.

653
00:26:21,113 --> 00:26:22,482
(Tom and Crow scoffing)

654
00:26:22,515 --> 00:26:24,117
(laughing) Look at
that piece of crap!

655
00:26:24,150 --> 00:26:26,485
What'd you use here, a
bowling pin, lacrosse helmet,

656
00:26:26,518 --> 00:26:28,488
some Tupperware panels, I--

657
00:26:28,522 --> 00:26:30,590
Wait a minute.

658
00:26:30,623 --> 00:26:31,892
(coughing)

659
00:26:31,925 --> 00:26:33,225
- Commercial sign.
- Oh.

660
00:26:33,258 --> 00:26:34,127
- Commercial sign.
- Oh yeah,

661
00:26:34,160 --> 00:26:35,262
commercial sign.

662
00:26:35,295 --> 00:26:37,063
(robots laughing and talking)

663
00:26:37,097 --> 00:26:43,235
("Love Theme from MST3K")

664
00:26:43,268 --> 00:26:44,570
(men talking)

665
00:26:44,603 --> 00:26:48,240
(robots whistling)
Whew, that was close.

666
00:26:48,273 --> 00:26:49,977
They all sent in "Northern
Exposure" spec scripts.

667
00:26:50,010 --> 00:26:51,778
That Munson's record is as
clean as mine, maybe cleaner.

668
00:26:51,812 --> 00:26:53,613
All three of them were
excellent security risks.

669
00:26:53,647 --> 00:26:55,148
It's a familiar pattern,

670
00:26:55,182 --> 00:26:57,283
top scientist engaged in
top level research work,

671
00:26:57,317 --> 00:27:00,120
suddenly walks off with
valuable data and materials.

672
00:27:00,153 --> 00:27:01,020
[Tom, Joel, And
Crow] Hugh Beaumont!

673
00:27:01,053 --> 00:27:02,355
But why?

674
00:27:02,388 --> 00:27:04,023
Who's behind all this?

675
00:27:04,057 --> 00:27:06,258
Well, at least we know
it's not the Russians.

676
00:27:06,292 --> 00:27:07,560
They already have the technology

677
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:09,262
to produce the same material.

678
00:27:09,295 --> 00:27:10,530
An Irving Thalberg award.

679
00:27:10,563 --> 00:27:11,898
- I know it's a long shot,
- Hmm.

680
00:27:11,932 --> 00:27:14,267
But I'm beginning to
suspect red China's our heavy.

681
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:17,237
The closest these have
ever been to anything Chinese

682
00:27:17,270 --> 00:27:19,105
is the local chop suey house.

683
00:27:19,138 --> 00:27:19,705
(Crow laughing)

684
00:27:19,738 --> 00:27:20,207
Shut up!

685
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:21,207
Then who?

686
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:22,608
We've got to come up
with something and quick,

687
00:27:22,642 --> 00:27:23,810
Washington's breathing
down my neck.

688
00:27:23,843 --> 00:27:24,877
The question is,

689
00:27:24,910 --> 00:27:27,313
which one of these
is gonna be next?

690
00:27:27,347 --> 00:27:29,915
Take your pick, they're
all above suspicion.

691
00:27:29,948 --> 00:27:32,785
I've had them under
surveillance for over a month.

692
00:27:32,818 --> 00:27:34,653
Oh, here's one

693
00:27:34,687 --> 00:27:36,655
I wouldn't mind having
under surveillance.

694
00:27:36,690 --> 00:27:39,425
That's my daughter,
you bastard.

695
00:27:39,458 --> 00:27:41,360
Dr. Lynn Young.

696
00:27:41,393 --> 00:27:42,695
- Yum yum.
- Clinical research

697
00:27:42,728 --> 00:27:44,197
division of Pacific
Laboratories.

698
00:27:44,230 --> 00:27:45,798
Oh, she's top drawer.

699
00:27:45,832 --> 00:27:47,800
She's here from Taiwan, as a
special favor to the nationals.

700
00:27:47,833 --> 00:27:49,702
(buzzer ringing)

701
00:27:49,735 --> 00:27:50,870
Yeah, what is it now?

702
00:27:50,903 --> 00:27:52,305
Miss Wilson is here

703
00:27:52,338 --> 00:27:53,473
with the reports
from the crime lab.

704
00:27:53,507 --> 00:27:56,175
- Ann or Nancy?
- Send her in.

705
00:27:56,208 --> 00:27:58,177
Our troubles are over.

706
00:27:58,210 --> 00:28:01,815
Gale Wilson, number one
undercover girl is on the case.

707
00:28:01,848 --> 00:28:02,882
It's Mary Kay Ash.

708
00:28:02,915 --> 00:28:05,285
You have an eye for talent,
Mr. Martin...

709
00:28:05,318 --> 00:28:06,720
- ...but that's about all.
- Jamming our radar.

710
00:28:06,753 --> 00:28:08,455
You're way off
track in this case.

711
00:28:08,488 --> 00:28:09,522
That's what I love
about your dear,

712
00:28:09,555 --> 00:28:11,758
your respect for your superiors.

713
00:28:11,792 --> 00:28:13,258
Alright, Gale, what'd you get?

714
00:28:13,292 --> 00:28:14,760
Adenoids.

715
00:28:14,793 --> 00:28:16,095
It seems Dr.
Munson did not die

716
00:28:16,128 --> 00:28:18,765
from a fall over the cliff.

717
00:28:18,798 --> 00:28:19,598
Are you serious?

718
00:28:19,632 --> 00:28:21,033
With that hairdo?

719
00:28:21,067 --> 00:28:22,468
Here's the coroner's report.

720
00:28:22,502 --> 00:28:24,203
The corn's report?

721
00:28:24,237 --> 00:28:26,572
First he was shot
then he wasn't shot.

722
00:28:26,605 --> 00:28:28,273
(audience groaning)

723
00:28:28,307 --> 00:28:30,610
Only he wasn't killed, I
suppose now we'll discover

724
00:28:30,643 --> 00:28:32,212
he died from an overdose
of sleeping pills.

725
00:28:32,245 --> 00:28:33,980
No, he was electrocuted.

726
00:28:34,013 --> 00:28:35,215
D'oh!

727
00:28:35,248 --> 00:28:36,282
Electrocuted?

728
00:28:36,315 --> 00:28:38,050
Now look here, don't
you play games with me,

729
00:28:38,083 --> 00:28:40,620
I'll have you transferred
back to Washington, Miss Hart?

730
00:28:40,653 --> 00:28:42,722
On the level,
see for yourself.

731
00:28:42,755 --> 00:28:44,990
Dr. Munson was dead
at least 12 hours

732
00:28:45,023 --> 00:28:46,625
before they found
him in the canyon.

733
00:28:46,658 --> 00:28:48,227
Look could we
please just not fight?

734
00:28:48,260 --> 00:28:49,862
- Found my house on here.
- Alright, Glenn

735
00:28:49,895 --> 00:28:51,530
Bite me.

736
00:28:51,563 --> 00:28:52,798
Suppose you tell me how a man

737
00:28:52,832 --> 00:28:54,633
who's been dead
for several hours

738
00:28:54,667 --> 00:28:56,970
can drive a car up a
mountain road, get out,

739
00:28:57,003 --> 00:28:58,037
and jump over a cliff?

740
00:28:58,070 --> 00:28:59,672
He's Jesus?

741
00:28:59,705 --> 00:29:00,907
He was murdered,
driven there by somebody,

742
00:29:00,940 --> 00:29:02,942
and thrown over the cliff.

743
00:29:02,975 --> 00:29:04,577
What a wonderful deduction!

744
00:29:04,610 --> 00:29:05,745
Thank you.

745
00:29:05,778 --> 00:29:07,313
You Mr. Cole or Mr. Martin?

746
00:29:07,347 --> 00:29:08,482
Cole.

747
00:29:08,515 --> 00:29:10,048
You should have
been a detective.

748
00:29:10,082 --> 00:29:11,117
D'oh!

749
00:29:11,150 --> 00:29:12,218
(Tom mimicking rimshot)

750
00:29:12,252 --> 00:29:13,453
Well I suppose we'll
discover the man who was shot

751
00:29:13,487 --> 00:29:16,422
by the guard at the research
lab was Dr. Munson's

752
00:29:16,455 --> 00:29:18,257
non-existent twin brother.

753
00:29:18,290 --> 00:29:19,458
I'm kidding, of course.

754
00:29:19,492 --> 00:29:21,193
See what the
research department has

755
00:29:21,227 --> 00:29:22,862
on the Dornheimer estate.

756
00:29:22,895 --> 00:29:25,298
It's a mansion on
the Palisades here.

757
00:29:25,332 --> 00:29:26,698
Professor Dornheimer?

758
00:29:26,732 --> 00:29:27,433
Who's he?

759
00:29:27,467 --> 00:29:28,200
A famous physicist.

760
00:29:28,233 --> 00:29:29,568
Your Prozac, sir.

761
00:29:29,602 --> 00:29:30,770
He used to be on
the space agency

762
00:29:30,803 --> 00:29:32,372
advisory board until recently.

763
00:29:32,405 --> 00:29:34,107
Now he's retired.

764
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:36,042
He acts as a
sort of a consultant

765
00:29:36,075 --> 00:29:37,543
to the younger generation.

766
00:29:37,577 --> 00:29:40,213
If a genius has a problem, he
goes to see the old professor.

767
00:29:40,247 --> 00:29:41,247
I'm checking out.

768
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:43,650
He'd know all the leading
scientists personally.

769
00:29:43,683 --> 00:29:45,385
Probably.
Why, what's on your mind?

770
00:29:45,418 --> 00:29:48,153
Well you just said it,
when a genius has a problem,

771
00:29:48,187 --> 00:29:49,922
he goes to see
the old professor.

772
00:29:49,955 --> 00:29:51,557
Hey, what about
our luncheon date?

773
00:29:51,590 --> 00:29:53,025
It is now a dinner date.

774
00:29:53,058 --> 00:29:54,627
I already have a dinner date.

775
00:29:54,660 --> 00:29:56,262
Break it.

776
00:29:56,295 --> 00:29:57,797
Geez, what a dickweed.

777
00:29:57,830 --> 00:29:59,698
That's what I love about him.

778
00:29:59,732 --> 00:30:02,568
He's so modest, so considerate,

779
00:30:02,602 --> 00:30:05,638
and so sure of himself.

780
00:30:05,672 --> 00:30:06,738
And so sure of you.

781
00:30:06,772 --> 00:30:07,973
Prozac?

782
00:30:08,007 --> 00:30:09,608
Guests of the
Human Duplicators

783
00:30:09,642 --> 00:30:11,878
stay at the Airport
Inn on the BeltLine.

784
00:30:11,912 --> 00:30:17,717
(ominous music)

785
00:30:17,750 --> 00:30:19,485
What's this?

786
00:30:19,518 --> 00:30:20,987
Donald O'Connor?

787
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:28,027
Driving Miss Daisy?

788
00:30:28,060 --> 00:30:29,962
I want you to follow
Mr. Rockford

789
00:30:29,995 --> 00:30:31,898
and see to it that he has
a serious accident

790
00:30:31,932 --> 00:30:34,867
that will take him off the case.
Permanently.

791
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:39,438
(Servo and Crow humming
"The Rockford Files" theme)

792
00:30:39,472 --> 00:30:43,075
(Servo mimics brakes squealing)

793
00:30:43,108 --> 00:30:45,043
♪ Mustang moves, Mustang grooves

794
00:30:45,077 --> 00:30:47,213
♪ Oh Mustang, Mustang '68

795
00:30:47,247 --> 00:30:53,385
(Joel and robots singing)

796
00:30:53,418 --> 00:30:54,487
(Tom gasping)

797
00:30:54,520 --> 00:30:56,355
He's driving to
Norma Desmond's house.

798
00:30:56,388 --> 00:31:01,727
(dramatic orchestral music)

799
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:06,933
Hey, quit trilling out here.

800
00:31:06,967 --> 00:31:10,770
It's kind of stuffy out
here in the garden today, and...

801
00:31:10,803 --> 00:31:13,740
Knew your father, I did.

802
00:31:13,773 --> 00:31:14,807
(ominous music)

803
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:16,775
Lisa.

804
00:31:16,808 --> 00:31:19,378
You have promised you are
not to enter the laboratory.

805
00:31:19,412 --> 00:31:21,380
But I have to go really bad.

806
00:31:21,413 --> 00:31:23,448
I want to know what's
going on down there.

807
00:31:23,482 --> 00:31:24,650
I have told you.

808
00:31:24,683 --> 00:31:27,153
We are conducting
important experiments.

809
00:31:27,187 --> 00:31:28,553
They are secret in nature.

810
00:31:28,588 --> 00:31:30,790
But why is everyone
so different?

811
00:31:30,823 --> 00:31:33,458
- Thor, the lab assistants,
- A little.

812
00:31:33,492 --> 00:31:35,928
even Uncle seems strange.

813
00:31:35,962 --> 00:31:38,063
You're only
imagining these things.

814
00:31:38,097 --> 00:31:39,032
I am not.

815
00:31:39,065 --> 00:31:40,567
Are too.

816
00:31:40,600 --> 00:31:41,867
Since you came here,
everything has been different.

817
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:42,702
Even my clothing.

818
00:31:42,735 --> 00:31:44,370
I'm not allowed to
leave the estate,

819
00:31:44,403 --> 00:31:46,872
I can't speak to visitors.

820
00:31:46,905 --> 00:31:48,540
Why, Kolos?

821
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,477
Why does everyone
take orders from you?

822
00:31:50,510 --> 00:31:53,747
Lisa, you must not defy me.

823
00:31:53,780 --> 00:31:55,848
(ominous music)

824
00:31:55,882 --> 00:31:57,383
I bet he doubles as a couch.

825
00:31:57,417 --> 00:31:59,385
(bell chiming)

826
00:31:59,418 --> 00:32:04,157
Oh, it's Efrem
Zimbalist Junior, Junior.

827
00:32:04,190 --> 00:32:05,592
Hi, are you familiar
with Greenpeace?

828
00:32:05,625 --> 00:32:06,625
Can I help you sir?

829
00:32:06,658 --> 00:32:08,293
Were you on
"Mission Impossible?"

830
00:32:08,327 --> 00:32:09,395
Yes, I'd like to see
Professor Dornheimer.

831
00:32:09,428 --> 00:32:10,297
(Tom laughing)

832
00:32:10,330 --> 00:32:12,132
Glenn Martin of View Magazine.

833
00:32:12,165 --> 00:32:14,467
We'd like to do a
feature article on him.

834
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:16,302
I will give the
professor your message.

835
00:32:16,335 --> 00:32:17,637
Please wait in here, sir.

836
00:32:17,670 --> 00:32:19,638
Thank you.

837
00:32:19,672 --> 00:32:22,208
(ominous music)

838
00:32:22,242 --> 00:32:25,077
Marilyn, you got another date!

839
00:32:25,110 --> 00:32:28,547
Hmm, nice, uh, stuff.

840
00:32:28,580 --> 00:32:30,717
Gosh, looks kinda like
my parents' living room.

841
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:35,555
- Really?
- Mm-hmm.

842
00:32:35,588 --> 00:32:39,692
Geez, I hate cold calling.

843
00:32:39,725 --> 00:32:41,927
Them.

844
00:32:41,960 --> 00:32:45,798
Little girl?

845
00:32:45,832 --> 00:32:47,633
Little girl, do you
always hide from guests?

846
00:32:47,667 --> 00:32:49,702
When they're as
unappealing as you, I do.

847
00:32:49,735 --> 00:32:51,370
I'm not a little girl.

848
00:32:51,403 --> 00:32:53,072
Oh, I should say
not, excuse me,

849
00:32:53,105 --> 00:32:56,508
I should have said Terpsichore.

850
00:32:56,542 --> 00:32:58,043
I should have said
(garbling words)

851
00:32:58,077 --> 00:32:59,478
- Were you rehearsing?
- Yes.

852
00:32:59,512 --> 00:33:00,547
I'm trying out
for "Ice Castles."

853
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:02,582
Are you a friend
of my uncle?

854
00:33:02,615 --> 00:33:04,550
If you mean Professor
Dornheimer, no,

855
00:33:04,583 --> 00:33:05,585
(audience laughing)

856
00:33:05,618 --> 00:33:06,252
I'm an admirer.

857
00:33:06,285 --> 00:33:08,788
Of fine silk products.

858
00:33:08,822 --> 00:33:12,125
Oh, I, uh, I believe
that's your scarf.

859
00:33:12,158 --> 00:33:13,092
I'm sick of picking up

860
00:33:13,125 --> 00:33:14,293
- your things, darling.
- Thank you.

861
00:33:14,327 --> 00:33:15,293
Mister?

862
00:33:15,327 --> 00:33:16,362
Martin.

863
00:33:16,395 --> 00:33:17,097
Glenn Martin.

864
00:33:17,130 --> 00:33:18,630
Glen Ross.

865
00:33:18,663 --> 00:33:21,133
Mr. Martin, (Tom
talking over Lisa)

866
00:33:21,167 --> 00:33:22,435
I can't tell you anymore but--

867
00:33:22,468 --> 00:33:23,568
(door opens)

868
00:33:23,602 --> 00:33:24,403
Mr. Martin?

869
00:33:24,437 --> 00:33:26,805
Vince Lombardi!

870
00:33:26,838 --> 00:33:29,308
Forgot my pants!

871
00:33:29,342 --> 00:33:31,077
Hobson's choice. (laughing)

872
00:33:31,110 --> 00:33:31,910
Professor Dornheimer.

873
00:33:31,943 --> 00:33:32,878
This is my hand.

874
00:33:32,912 --> 00:33:33,880
This is an honor for me.

875
00:33:33,913 --> 00:33:36,248
There it is and still, ah.

876
00:33:36,282 --> 00:33:38,885
(robots mimicking buzzer)

877
00:33:38,918 --> 00:33:41,587
There's no need to
be formal, Mr. Martin.

878
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:42,588
Please, take off your pants.

879
00:33:42,622 --> 00:33:44,757
I am a simple man.

880
00:33:44,790 --> 00:33:48,293
You wish to do some
kind of paper, I believe?

881
00:33:48,327 --> 00:33:49,962
Yes, a feature article.

882
00:33:49,995 --> 00:33:51,530
I'd like to give
the public a view

883
00:33:51,563 --> 00:33:56,302
into the... into the daily life
of a great scientist.

884
00:33:56,335 --> 00:33:57,303
As you have already seen,

885
00:33:57,337 --> 00:33:58,805
(Tom gasping)

886
00:33:58,838 --> 00:34:01,407
my life is not the dull routine

887
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:03,877
the general public
might imagine.

888
00:34:03,910 --> 00:34:05,712
No, your niece is lovely.

889
00:34:05,745 --> 00:34:07,647
What the hell is
that supposed to mean?

890
00:34:07,680 --> 00:34:08,982
Not perfect.

891
00:34:09,015 --> 00:34:15,320
("Love Theme from MST3K")

892
00:34:15,353 --> 00:34:19,492
What is it that you wanted to
know about me, Mr. Martin?

893
00:34:19,525 --> 00:34:20,593
Do you like to wear mittens?

894
00:34:20,627 --> 00:34:21,693
I understand that
you often give advice

895
00:34:21,727 --> 00:34:24,030
to some of our
leading scientists.

896
00:34:24,063 --> 00:34:24,963
I try to give help.

897
00:34:24,997 --> 00:34:26,098
But look at me!

898
00:34:26,132 --> 00:34:28,033
Knowledge should be available

899
00:34:28,067 --> 00:34:29,302
to those who know
how to use it.

900
00:34:29,335 --> 00:34:30,803
Are they speaking in code?

901
00:34:30,837 --> 00:34:34,707
Then your experience is not
available to all scientists?

902
00:34:34,740 --> 00:34:35,808
Certainly not.

903
00:34:35,842 --> 00:34:37,310
I deny them my essence.

904
00:34:37,343 --> 00:34:39,245
Knowledge, especially
advanced knowledge,

905
00:34:39,278 --> 00:34:43,448
should be entrusted to a
select few superior minds.

906
00:34:43,482 --> 00:34:44,817
Oh, I see.

907
00:34:44,850 --> 00:34:46,952
Philosophy like that could
make a pretty wide gap

908
00:34:46,985 --> 00:34:50,055
in human understanding, we'd
end up with a civilization

909
00:34:50,088 --> 00:34:52,925
of extremes, the few
who have everything,

910
00:34:52,958 --> 00:34:54,560
the rest who have nothing.

911
00:34:54,593 --> 00:34:55,862
Sounds like the
Reagan-Bush years.

912
00:34:55,895 --> 00:34:57,297
Each of us has his own
philosophy, Mr. Martin,

913
00:34:57,330 --> 00:34:58,530
I like pancakes.

914
00:34:58,563 --> 00:35:01,200
as long as he is able
to think for himself.

915
00:35:01,233 --> 00:35:04,037
Yes, well that's true.

916
00:35:04,070 --> 00:35:05,472
Any more booze over here?

917
00:35:05,505 --> 00:35:07,207
(snoring)

918
00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:09,375
I understand that you knew
Doctors Danziger and LaSatier.

919
00:35:09,408 --> 00:35:10,408
Is that right?

920
00:35:10,442 --> 00:35:11,477
What about Estelle Getty?

921
00:35:11,510 --> 00:35:12,145
Oh. (clears throat)

922
00:35:12,178 --> 00:35:13,645
Yes of course.

923
00:35:13,678 --> 00:35:15,413
Why do you ask?

924
00:35:15,447 --> 00:35:17,583
Were they members of your
so-called select group?

925
00:35:17,617 --> 00:35:19,118
I oughta kick
your so-called butt.

926
00:35:19,152 --> 00:35:21,387
Or would that be called
"confidential informstion"?

927
00:35:21,420 --> 00:35:22,822
Give me my drink back.

928
00:35:22,855 --> 00:35:26,158
It might be considered that.

929
00:35:26,192 --> 00:35:28,060
Ah, my other niece.

930
00:35:28,093 --> 00:35:30,028
Dr. Kolos wishes
to speak to you.

931
00:35:30,062 --> 00:35:31,730
Thank you, Thor.

932
00:35:31,763 --> 00:35:33,232
Will you excuse me for a
few moments, Mr. Martin?

933
00:35:33,265 --> 00:35:34,267
Certainly.

934
00:35:34,300 --> 00:35:36,235
Okay if I play bumper pool?

935
00:35:36,268 --> 00:35:38,370
I guess not, huh?

936
00:35:38,403 --> 00:35:42,942
Alone with the booze, ha ha!

937
00:35:42,975 --> 00:35:45,812
How 'bout a little
harp solo, huh?

938
00:35:45,845 --> 00:35:49,548
Who do you think
decorated here, Miss Kitty?

939
00:35:49,582 --> 00:35:52,952
Somebody's tipping
the light fantastic.

940
00:35:52,985 --> 00:35:54,387
It's Marty Feldman!

941
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:56,222
Hey, where'd
he, what the heck,

942
00:35:56,255 --> 00:35:57,857
oh, oh there he is.

943
00:35:57,890 --> 00:36:01,493
(dramatic music)

944
00:36:01,527 --> 00:36:03,628
This man, a
tyrannical dictator

945
00:36:03,662 --> 00:36:07,233
is in love with this man
who is love with this man

946
00:36:07,267 --> 00:36:11,137
who is played by Peter Lupus.

947
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:13,438
Uh oh. (clears
throat and whistles)

948
00:36:13,472 --> 00:36:14,907
Nothing, nothing, lampshade,

949
00:36:14,940 --> 00:36:15,908
oh, do you have any olives?

950
00:36:15,942 --> 00:36:19,245
Looking for olives,
couldn't find any olives.

951
00:36:19,278 --> 00:36:22,047
Professor Dornheimer
sends his apologies.

952
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:24,450
He suggested you
call at a later date.

953
00:36:24,483 --> 00:36:25,918
Oh, let's belly bump, come on.

954
00:36:25,952 --> 00:36:27,220
Right here.

955
00:36:27,253 --> 00:36:28,420
Thank the professor
for the drink.

956
00:36:28,453 --> 00:36:31,223
Oh, that's a fentoozler, you
get to keep the glass.

957
00:36:31,257 --> 00:36:32,992
Oh, geez, come
here to do a story,

958
00:36:33,025 --> 00:36:36,295
all I get is a cheap draw
scotch, probably Cutty.

959
00:36:36,328 --> 00:36:38,097
It's a very Brady Renaissance.

960
00:36:38,130 --> 00:36:43,702
(dramatic music)

961
00:36:43,735 --> 00:36:45,872
Well, on the other hand,
I did get a free drink.

962
00:36:45,905 --> 00:36:49,108
Ah, I'll come back tomorrow,
see if they remember me.

963
00:36:49,142 --> 00:36:51,210
(Tom singing to self)

964
00:36:51,243 --> 00:36:52,712
Hey, what the hey,

965
00:36:52,745 --> 00:36:54,213
how do you work an
automatic, anyway?

966
00:36:54,247 --> 00:36:57,983
(dramatic music)

967
00:36:58,017 --> 00:36:59,652
What's this?

968
00:36:59,685 --> 00:37:02,822
Dewlap man following our hero?

969
00:37:02,855 --> 00:37:06,258
I gotta coast down the
driveway to get it started.

970
00:37:06,292 --> 00:37:10,428
(mimicking sputtering car)

971
00:37:10,462 --> 00:37:11,763
Ooooh!

972
00:37:11,797 --> 00:37:13,298
Ow!

973
00:37:13,332 --> 00:37:14,467
(phone ringing)

974
00:37:14,500 --> 00:37:15,735
Oh, your dog's ringing.

975
00:37:15,768 --> 00:37:19,305
Oh, it's the phone.

976
00:37:19,338 --> 00:37:21,073
Well it's about time.

977
00:37:21,107 --> 00:37:22,742
Where are you?

978
00:37:22,775 --> 00:37:23,875
I'm on a sound stage.

979
00:37:23,908 --> 00:37:26,245
A cozy little hideaway.
Wish you were here.

980
00:37:26,278 --> 00:37:29,715
Glenn, how'd
the conference go?

981
00:37:29,748 --> 00:37:33,418
I'd say the corporation
is on shaky ground.

982
00:37:33,452 --> 00:37:36,422
What did the research department
turn up on our friend?

983
00:37:36,455 --> 00:37:39,658
Oh, he's considered a
good citizen and all that.

984
00:37:39,692 --> 00:37:41,393
(laughing) You
should hear his ideas

985
00:37:41,427 --> 00:37:43,262
about all men being equal.

986
00:37:43,295 --> 00:37:45,430
Well, he's supposed to
be a little eccentric.

987
00:37:45,463 --> 00:37:48,433
And in scientific circles,
his books are considered--

988
00:37:48,467 --> 00:37:51,037
He's calling from the "I
Dream of Jeannie" credits.

989
00:37:51,070 --> 00:37:52,237
About our dinner date,

990
00:37:52,270 --> 00:37:53,572
Are you asking me?

991
00:37:53,605 --> 00:37:55,140
(robots yelling)
Or are you telling me?

992
00:37:55,173 --> 00:37:56,675
Anything you like,

993
00:37:56,708 --> 00:37:58,410
as long as it's the
Villa at eight.

994
00:37:58,443 --> 00:38:01,247
You devil, you know
how I love that place.

995
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:03,148
How could I refuse?

996
00:38:03,182 --> 00:38:04,483
I didn't expect you to.

997
00:38:04,517 --> 00:38:07,587
Oh and Gale, on your
way, pass by the library

998
00:38:07,620 --> 00:38:09,055
and get me copies
of Mr. D's books.

999
00:38:09,088 --> 00:38:10,422
See you at eight.

1000
00:38:10,455 --> 00:38:11,323
(laughing)

1001
00:38:11,357 --> 00:38:18,030
Zinged her.

1002
00:38:18,063 --> 00:38:19,132
(sudden dramatic music)

1003
00:38:19,165 --> 00:38:19,865
What's this?

1004
00:38:19,898 --> 00:38:20,632
- Okay.
- Henry Sil--

1005
00:38:20,667 --> 00:38:22,200
Oh, oh, sorry.

1006
00:38:22,233 --> 00:38:23,068
(exciting music)

1007
00:38:23,102 --> 00:38:28,607
America.
Building cities, drinking booze.

1008
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:29,942
Say.

1009
00:38:29,975 --> 00:38:33,045
Say!

1010
00:38:33,078 --> 00:38:35,982
Large club?

1011
00:38:36,015 --> 00:38:39,718
♪ We serve fun at
Shakey's, also pizza

1012
00:38:39,752 --> 00:38:41,120
Another bottle, Mr. Martin?

1013
00:38:41,153 --> 00:38:42,655
What are you, nuts?

1014
00:38:42,688 --> 00:38:44,323
No thanks, Gino, just
the check, please.

1015
00:38:44,357 --> 00:38:46,392
Yes, sir.

1016
00:38:46,425 --> 00:38:48,227
Uhh, I gotta go
into the bathroom

1017
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:49,728
and get the gun
out of the toilet.

1018
00:38:49,762 --> 00:38:51,030
Remember me?

1019
00:38:51,063 --> 00:38:52,898
I'm the girl you took to dinner.

1020
00:38:52,932 --> 00:38:53,698
I'm sorry.

1021
00:38:53,732 --> 00:38:54,467
You changed your hair.

1022
00:38:54,500 --> 00:38:56,135
I was someplace else.

1023
00:38:56,168 --> 00:38:57,335
You look like
you've got troubles.

1024
00:38:57,370 --> 00:38:59,072
Come on, let's talk about them.

1025
00:38:59,105 --> 00:39:00,840
What do you call this?

1026
00:39:00,873 --> 00:39:03,542
Gale, there's something
weird going on out there,

1027
00:39:03,575 --> 00:39:05,945
but I--
I can't put my finger on it.

1028
00:39:05,978 --> 00:39:07,245
Well if you don't
know what it is,

1029
00:39:07,278 --> 00:39:09,013
don't put your finger on it!

1030
00:39:09,048 --> 00:39:09,915
- Zing.
- Well I could

1031
00:39:09,948 --> 00:39:11,450
at the library.

1032
00:39:11,483 --> 00:39:14,287
It seems the science students in
this town really dig Mr. D.

1033
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:16,722
Hey, Mr. D.

1034
00:39:16,755 --> 00:39:17,790
Best regards, bit part.

1035
00:39:17,823 --> 00:39:18,890
There you go, thank you Gino.

1036
00:39:18,923 --> 00:39:19,958
Thank you sir.

1037
00:39:19,992 --> 00:39:22,227
Come on, let's
do some research.

1038
00:39:22,260 --> 00:39:23,262
Tonight?

1039
00:39:23,295 --> 00:39:25,263
(Tom laughing)
Tonight.

1040
00:39:25,297 --> 00:39:26,432
Oh, I thought we'd--

1041
00:39:26,465 --> 00:39:28,067
Heads we do, tails we don't.

1042
00:39:28,100 --> 00:39:30,702
It's an Etruscan penny.

1043
00:39:30,737 --> 00:39:35,908
Luck be a lady tonight, oh.

1044
00:39:35,942 --> 00:39:38,110
Come on, we need the chair.

1045
00:39:38,143 --> 00:39:42,080
(upbeat music)

1046
00:39:42,113 --> 00:39:44,483
(Crow singing to music)

1047
00:39:44,517 --> 00:39:49,288
♪ Please mister,
please, don't play C17

1048
00:39:49,322 --> 00:39:51,457
I hate that I know that.

1049
00:39:51,490 --> 00:39:55,227
Jim Henson's Jack
Palance Babies.

1050
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:57,395
Ah, Phil Harris
and Bubbles Rothermere

1051
00:39:57,430 --> 00:40:00,465
in the background, for
those playing along at home.

1052
00:40:00,498 --> 00:40:03,035
You guys think that
Phil Harris drank?

1053
00:40:03,068 --> 00:40:04,437
No.

1054
00:40:04,470 --> 00:40:08,473
- No?
- Uh, I don't know.

1055
00:40:08,507 --> 00:40:10,308
Look, why don't you
just do it in the bus tub,

1056
00:40:10,342 --> 00:40:12,143
for crying out loud?

1057
00:40:12,177 --> 00:40:16,282
Now, right now.

1058
00:40:16,315 --> 00:40:19,083
I crap bigger than them.

1059
00:40:19,118 --> 00:40:21,453
Visit Syria?

1060
00:40:21,487 --> 00:40:26,425
(robots singing upbeat tune)

1061
00:40:26,458 --> 00:40:30,797
Hey Bob, is this
raw bacon for me?

1062
00:40:30,830 --> 00:40:33,598
That's a Pedro Almodovar film.

1063
00:40:33,632 --> 00:40:36,902
He's doing a translation
of the Kama Sutra.

1064
00:40:36,935 --> 00:40:37,737
Glenn?

1065
00:40:37,770 --> 00:40:38,470
(Crow mocking Gale's voice)

1066
00:40:38,503 --> 00:40:41,140
One thing I don't understand,

1067
00:40:41,173 --> 00:40:44,243
If Professor Dornheimer's the
mastermind of these robberies,

1068
00:40:44,277 --> 00:40:46,278
what is he doing
with the materials?

1069
00:40:46,312 --> 00:40:47,613
You think he's
shipping them abroad?

1070
00:40:47,647 --> 00:40:49,415
Why do I sound
like Polly Holiday?

1071
00:40:49,448 --> 00:40:51,317
Glenn, I'm talking to you.

1072
00:40:51,350 --> 00:40:52,117
Yeah, I thought so.

1073
00:40:52,150 --> 00:40:53,552
Well, say something.

1074
00:40:53,585 --> 00:40:54,320
Something.

1075
00:40:54,353 --> 00:40:55,253
Something.

1076
00:40:55,287 --> 00:40:56,355
D'oh!

1077
00:40:56,388 --> 00:40:57,255
- D'oh.
- Sometimes I wonder

1078
00:40:57,288 --> 00:40:58,190
what I see in you.

1079
00:40:58,223 --> 00:40:59,492
Got riff back on that.

1080
00:40:59,525 --> 00:41:00,425
(Glenn talking)

1081
00:41:00,458 --> 00:41:01,860
Me.

1082
00:41:01,893 --> 00:41:04,597
Gale, what do you know
about cybernetics?

1083
00:41:04,630 --> 00:41:05,563
Cyber who?

1084
00:41:05,597 --> 00:41:07,198
Cybernetics.

1085
00:41:07,232 --> 00:41:08,967
It's the study of
calculators and computers,

1086
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:13,072
all kinds of mechanical
devices to control automation.

1087
00:41:13,105 --> 00:41:14,105
Mechanical brains.

1088
00:41:14,138 --> 00:41:16,275
So, cybernetics?

1089
00:41:16,308 --> 00:41:19,745
So, Dornheimer is the
chief exponent in the field.

1090
00:41:19,778 --> 00:41:22,047
He's the father of
the thinking machine.

1091
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:23,115
Come on, jump
over the couch now.

1092
00:41:23,148 --> 00:41:24,317
That's the trouble today.

1093
00:41:24,350 --> 00:41:25,885
It's getting to the
point where people

1094
00:41:25,918 --> 00:41:28,120
hardly have a chance
to think for themselves.

1095
00:41:28,153 --> 00:41:29,120
Could you not talk?

1096
00:41:29,153 --> 00:41:29,888
I don't get the connection.

1097
00:41:29,922 --> 00:41:30,890
Please?

1098
00:41:30,923 --> 00:41:31,957
You just said it.

1099
00:41:31,990 --> 00:41:33,092
I did?

1100
00:41:33,125 --> 00:41:35,427
Yeah, listen to
what Dornheimer says.

1101
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:38,097
"And there's every reason
to believe that in time

1102
00:41:38,130 --> 00:41:39,765
"man will devise a method
whereby

1103
00:41:39,798 --> 00:41:41,433
"a mechanical brain system

1104
00:41:41,467 --> 00:41:44,937
"will be linked electronically
with a human nervous system

1105
00:41:44,970 --> 00:41:48,573
thereby creating a totally
independent thinking machine."

1106
00:41:48,607 --> 00:41:49,908
That'd be me, pretty much.

1107
00:41:49,942 --> 00:41:51,577
"Once this phase
has been accomplished,

1108
00:41:51,610 --> 00:41:54,480
"it will only be a short
step to the creation

1109
00:41:54,513 --> 00:41:56,815
of the ultimate in
mechanical devices,"

1110
00:41:56,848 --> 00:41:57,650
Me.

1111
00:41:57,683 --> 00:41:58,550
- "The android."
- No, me.

1112
00:41:58,583 --> 00:41:59,352
- No, me.
- Me.

1113
00:41:59,385 --> 00:42:00,185
- An android?
- Me.

1114
00:42:00,218 --> 00:42:01,187
Me.

1115
00:42:01,220 --> 00:42:02,087
What's an android?

1116
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:04,723
An android dear, is a robot,

1117
00:42:04,757 --> 00:42:06,425
like a mechanical man.

1118
00:42:06,458 --> 00:42:07,727
Like my acting.

1119
00:42:07,760 --> 00:42:10,128
Except with one
important difference.

1120
00:42:10,162 --> 00:42:13,298
An android would look
exactly like a human being.

1121
00:42:13,332 --> 00:42:14,900
Except for the huge
bolts in the neck.

1122
00:42:14,933 --> 00:42:17,102
An android that looks
like a human being?

1123
00:42:17,135 --> 00:42:18,570
Like you or me?

1124
00:42:18,603 --> 00:42:21,973
Or Wells, or...
or Dr. Munson.

1125
00:42:22,007 --> 00:42:24,410
Oh, that's the wildest
thing I ever heard of.

1126
00:42:24,443 --> 00:42:26,712
Let's drink, then love.

1127
00:42:26,745 --> 00:42:28,780
Glenn, you don't believe
that's possible, do you?

1128
00:42:28,813 --> 00:42:30,448
Excuse me, I'm gonna go
toss myself out the window here.

1129
00:42:30,482 --> 00:42:31,150
Glenn?

1130
00:42:31,183 --> 00:42:32,283
(Tom mimicking glass breaking)

1131
00:42:32,317 --> 00:42:34,487
You don't believe that
Professor Dornheimer

1132
00:42:34,520 --> 00:42:36,822
has actually done this, do you?

1133
00:42:36,855 --> 00:42:39,325
Gale, all through
history there have been men

1134
00:42:39,358 --> 00:42:41,260
with minds like Dornheimer's.

1135
00:42:41,293 --> 00:42:42,862
Like Dornheimer died?

1136
00:42:42,895 --> 00:42:45,363
Men like that can go as far
as their minds can reach.

1137
00:42:45,397 --> 00:42:46,532
Anything they can see

1138
00:42:46,565 --> 00:42:48,067
I can see better.

1139
00:42:48,100 --> 00:42:49,535
(Tom laughing)

1140
00:42:49,568 --> 00:42:51,437
So, why not androids?

1141
00:42:51,470 --> 00:42:57,743
("Love Theme from MST3K")

1142
00:42:57,777 --> 00:43:00,545
Oh, this is beautiful.

1143
00:43:00,578 --> 00:43:02,915
That's me, and hey, that's me,

1144
00:43:02,948 --> 00:43:05,750
and look over there,
there I am! (laughing)

1145
00:43:05,783 --> 00:43:07,285
You know, there's a
whole lot of me in here,

1146
00:43:07,318 --> 00:43:09,888
truckloads of me
and more on the way.

1147
00:43:09,922 --> 00:43:12,625
Hi Tom.
Well, hello there Tom,

1148
00:43:12,658 --> 00:43:14,660
you're looking
pretty dapper today.

1149
00:43:14,693 --> 00:43:16,928
Oh, he's looking sickly.

1150
00:43:16,962 --> 00:43:19,230
Well you're gonna get that,
lose two or three a day,

1151
00:43:19,263 --> 00:43:22,768
it's just part of the business,
you can't get too attached.

1152
00:43:22,802 --> 00:43:24,770
That's why it's
evil to duplicate,

1153
00:43:24,803 --> 00:43:26,972
everyone's an individual and
that's why it's a sacrilege

1154
00:43:27,005 --> 00:43:30,475
to think that you have
the right to... oh my God!

1155
00:43:30,508 --> 00:43:32,577
Easy fellas, easy, it's okay.

1156
00:43:32,610 --> 00:43:33,645
Hi guys, guess what?

1157
00:43:33,678 --> 00:43:35,480
Tom, what have you done?

1158
00:43:35,513 --> 00:43:37,048
Oh!

1159
00:43:37,082 --> 00:43:39,318
Just playing God, it's
going pretty well. (laughing)

1160
00:43:39,352 --> 00:43:40,987
Tom, you made dozens
and dozens of yourself,

1161
00:43:41,020 --> 00:43:42,655
I don't think that's right.

1162
00:43:42,688 --> 00:43:44,088
You know, you brought
up some very serious

1163
00:43:44,122 --> 00:43:46,325
psycho-moral
issues here, buster.

1164
00:43:46,358 --> 00:43:48,493
Frankly, I don't
see a problem, Joel.

1165
00:43:48,527 --> 00:43:50,362
I've just surrounded myself
with an endless horde

1166
00:43:50,395 --> 00:43:53,065
of mindless disciples ready
to go forth and do my bidding!

1167
00:43:53,098 --> 00:43:54,767
I guess I don't see a downside.

1168
00:43:54,800 --> 00:43:56,335
How'd you do it anyway?

1169
00:43:56,368 --> 00:43:57,903
Oh, you know, simple prototype,

1170
00:43:57,937 --> 00:44:01,040
Japanese management style,
just-in-time stock procedures.

1171
00:44:01,073 --> 00:44:03,608
Oh hey, we've got more out
back, where do you want 'em?

1172
00:44:03,642 --> 00:44:05,043
Uh, just make sure
they're all signed in.

1173
00:44:05,077 --> 00:44:06,445
Roger.

1174
00:44:06,478 --> 00:44:08,713
Oh, Tom, listen, this is wrong.

1175
00:44:08,747 --> 00:44:11,617
I'm not gonna take care of
'em, I'm not gonna feed 'em,

1176
00:44:11,650 --> 00:44:13,152
and keep 'em out of
the kitchen, okay?

1177
00:44:13,185 --> 00:44:13,918
(Tom sighing)

1178
00:44:13,952 --> 00:44:15,153
Tom, Tom, Tom.

1179
00:44:15,187 --> 00:44:16,388
Once again you
really didn't think

1180
00:44:16,422 --> 00:44:17,723
this thing through, did ya?

1181
00:44:17,757 --> 00:44:21,060
Like you're so cool that
we need more of you.

1182
00:44:21,093 --> 00:44:23,295
Oh, cynics, huh?

1183
00:44:23,328 --> 00:44:24,863
Alright, Toms, attack!

1184
00:44:24,897 --> 00:44:26,065
Tear 'em apart, get 'em!

1185
00:44:26,098 --> 00:44:27,800
Rip 'em apart!

1186
00:44:27,833 --> 00:44:29,435
Come on, attack, come on,
you're embarrassing me.

1187
00:44:29,468 --> 00:44:30,568
(Crow laughing)
Tom.

1188
00:44:30,602 --> 00:44:32,403
Joel.

1189
00:44:32,437 --> 00:44:33,905
I guess you were right.

1190
00:44:33,938 --> 00:44:37,275
It is wrong to play God, I've
certainly learned my lesson.

1191
00:44:37,308 --> 00:44:40,945
I know one guy that needs
a time out. (trails off)

1192
00:44:40,978 --> 00:44:41,813
(Tom crying)

1193
00:44:41,847 --> 00:44:43,048
Well, what a lame thing.

1194
00:44:43,082 --> 00:44:44,048
(sighs)

1195
00:44:44,082 --> 00:44:44,783
Hey, hi.

1196
00:44:44,817 --> 00:44:45,617
You're kind of cute.

1197
00:44:45,650 --> 00:44:46,985
Oh, Joel?

1198
00:44:47,018 --> 00:44:48,053
(laughing)

1199
00:44:48,087 --> 00:44:49,120
Hey, what the, hey cut that out!

1200
00:44:49,153 --> 00:44:50,322
(alarm buzzing)

1201
00:44:50,355 --> 00:44:51,490
We got movie sign!

1202
00:44:51,523 --> 00:44:54,393
(screaming)

1203
00:44:54,427 --> 00:44:59,163
(doors creak and whir)

1204
00:44:59,197 --> 00:45:00,565
Just don't do it anymore.

1205
00:45:00,598 --> 00:45:02,500
I just don't want you--
just stop, okay?

1206
00:45:02,533 --> 00:45:05,737
Oh my god, oh it's terrible.

1207
00:45:05,770 --> 00:45:08,073
I'm with him.

1208
00:45:08,107 --> 00:45:10,875
Well.

1209
00:45:10,908 --> 00:45:14,780
Oh great, the
souvenir shop's closed.

1210
00:45:14,813 --> 00:45:17,750
Hey, give someone else
a chance at that ball.

1211
00:45:17,783 --> 00:45:20,818
Man he's got a real
cute shape on him.

1212
00:45:20,852 --> 00:45:22,253
I'm actually kind
of glad it's locked,

1213
00:45:22,287 --> 00:45:23,255
I'm pretty tired.

1214
00:45:23,288 --> 00:45:24,957
Now I can knock off.

1215
00:45:24,990 --> 00:45:28,227
I'm Hans Christian Andersen.

1216
00:45:28,260 --> 00:45:35,500
♪ Timothy, Timothy, hungry
as hell no food to eat.

1217
00:45:35,533 --> 00:45:37,002
Well here's something

1218
00:45:37,035 --> 00:45:37,937
that never happened
in his career.

1219
00:45:37,970 --> 00:45:42,240
(mimics drum roll)

1220
00:45:42,273 --> 00:45:44,910
Hey, it's got a loft!
Wow!

1221
00:45:44,943 --> 00:45:48,680
And torsos? Cool!

1222
00:45:48,713 --> 00:45:53,518
- Tony Bennett!
- ♪ I left my head...

1223
00:45:53,552 --> 00:45:56,388
Boy, if a fellow happened to be
a human duplicator,

1224
00:45:56,422 --> 00:46:01,627
he could have a ball up here!

1225
00:46:01,660 --> 00:46:03,362
Now, are these
natural formations?

1226
00:46:03,395 --> 00:46:05,930
Sacred miracle cave.

1227
00:46:05,963 --> 00:46:08,100
(laughing)

1228
00:46:08,133 --> 00:46:11,503
Say, nice gams.

1229
00:46:11,537 --> 00:46:14,105
Looks like he broke into
the display at Chess King.

1230
00:46:14,138 --> 00:46:15,173
(laughs)

1231
00:46:15,207 --> 00:46:20,045
Richard Kiel's got a
butt collection.

1232
00:46:20,078 --> 00:46:22,713
Tom Jones!
And Peter Garrett!

1233
00:46:22,747 --> 00:46:24,683
What do you know.

1234
00:46:24,717 --> 00:46:31,257
Put me down.

1235
00:46:31,290 --> 00:46:33,025
Uh, excuse me, do you work here?

1236
00:46:33,058 --> 00:46:35,027
Oh, no.

1237
00:46:35,060 --> 00:46:36,195
Mark Twain.

1238
00:46:36,228 --> 00:46:40,032
No, it's Hal
Holbrook, actually.

1239
00:46:40,065 --> 00:46:43,535
Look, no one's going
to tell you you need Clearasil.

1240
00:46:43,568 --> 00:46:45,103
Oh, just looking
for the bathroom.

1241
00:46:45,137 --> 00:46:47,873
Nothing, nothing. Oh.

1242
00:46:47,907 --> 00:46:50,775
Hold me wall, hold me tight.

1243
00:46:50,808 --> 00:46:53,578
If you don't move, you're
invisible. Remember that.

1244
00:46:53,612 --> 00:46:59,652
Mmm? Hmm.

1245
00:46:59,685 --> 00:47:08,460
Just in time, I'm starved.

1246
00:47:08,493 --> 00:47:12,530
How come I never get
nurses like that?

1247
00:47:12,563 --> 00:47:15,367
Hey, who's playing
that contrabassoon?

1248
00:47:15,400 --> 00:47:16,368
Ollie North?

1249
00:47:16,402 --> 00:47:24,042
Contra-bassoon.

1250
00:47:24,075 --> 00:47:27,078
Where angels go,
trouble follows.

1251
00:47:28,880 --> 00:47:30,348
Dom, we'll be back
in an a half an hour,

1252
00:47:30,382 --> 00:47:35,420
Mr. Deluise.

1253
00:47:35,453 --> 00:47:37,455
Well, back for another load.

1254
00:47:37,488 --> 00:47:39,725
You know, for nurses,
they're not very speedy.

1255
00:47:39,758 --> 00:47:46,698
That's why they
work in a morgue.

1256
00:47:46,732 --> 00:47:49,000
Hey they're hickory
smoking people in here.

1257
00:47:49,033 --> 00:47:50,335
Oh, fellow must
have been Jewish,

1258
00:47:50,368 --> 00:47:52,003
they buried him in a steam bath.

1259
00:47:52,037 --> 00:47:55,940
Zing.

1260
00:47:55,973 --> 00:47:58,143
Judy Carne?

1261
00:47:58,177 --> 00:48:06,083
I'm up, I'm up.

1262
00:48:06,117 --> 00:48:07,953
Too much porcellana.

1263
00:48:07,987 --> 00:48:12,390
Too bad, that's
her throwing hand.

1264
00:48:12,423 --> 00:48:15,027
(muffled) Hey, I'm in here.

1265
00:48:15,060 --> 00:48:21,500
Martin Sheen.

1266
00:48:21,533 --> 00:48:26,772
Then he found one
that was just right.

1267
00:48:26,805 --> 00:48:28,540
Ew, that guy's ripe.

1268
00:48:28,573 --> 00:48:31,577
Ah, I think there's a
pattern developing here.

1269
00:48:31,610 --> 00:48:35,547
There's people in these things.

1270
00:48:35,580 --> 00:48:39,250
Uh, this one's gone over.

1271
00:48:39,283 --> 00:48:44,022
They should label those.

1272
00:48:44,055 --> 00:48:46,392
Yeah, you know it's always
the last place you look.

1273
00:48:46,425 --> 00:48:49,127
The Doctor will see you now.

1274
00:48:49,160 --> 00:48:51,330
Shattered, shattered.

1275
00:48:51,363 --> 00:48:55,267
Shadoobie...
Shatter.

1276
00:48:55,300 --> 00:48:56,368
Clean up in aisle three.

1277
00:48:56,402 --> 00:48:57,002
(laughs)

1278
00:48:57,035 --> 00:48:57,768
I'm kidding.

1279
00:48:57,802 --> 00:48:58,603
Zing.

1280
00:48:58,637 --> 00:49:00,705
Cause...

1281
00:49:00,738 --> 00:49:05,943
Well, that was a cool exhibit.
What's next, I wonder.

1282
00:49:05,977 --> 00:49:10,715
Tickets please, tickets
please, tickets please.

1283
00:49:10,748 --> 00:49:15,753
Well he's a queer duck.

1284
00:49:15,787 --> 00:49:18,623
Huh, the Guthrie Lab.

1285
00:49:18,657 --> 00:49:23,395
A wall of ginger snap.

1286
00:49:23,428 --> 00:49:27,732
It's Malibu Barbie
torture chambers.

1287
00:49:27,765 --> 00:49:29,768
Hi I am new Asian Barbie.

1288
00:49:34,638 --> 00:49:37,108
That was great pink
lady, we're going

1289
00:49:37,142 --> 00:49:42,547
to lay down the
rhythm track now.

1290
00:49:42,580 --> 00:49:46,117
Hey, I have no idea
what I'm doing here.

1291
00:49:46,150 --> 00:49:50,355
And the phone lines
are going crazy.

1292
00:49:50,388 --> 00:49:53,425
Let's take it from the top,
a little more bass this time.

1293
00:49:53,458 --> 00:49:55,360
It stinks.

1294
00:49:55,393 --> 00:49:58,430
I'm going to take my
lunch break now, excuse me.

1295
00:49:58,463 --> 00:49:59,430
Oh, she always takes her break

1296
00:49:59,463 --> 00:50:01,767
no matter what--
Doctor.

1297
00:50:01,800 --> 00:50:07,538
♪ Oh, dream weaver,
